"BundleFusion (https://graphics.stanford.edu/projects/bundlefusion/index.html)\nciteturn290view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn288view4\",\"id\":0}); Total lines: 191\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn290view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn288view1\",\"pattern\":\"update\"}); Total lines: 1\nNo matching text found for \"update\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirect Sparse Odometry (https://arxiv.org/html/1607.02565v2)\nciteturn290view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn288view2\",\"pattern\":\"Levenberg\"}); Total lines: 613\nL205:  | $\\displaystyle r_{k}$  | $\\displaystyle=r_{k}(\\boldsymbol{x}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0})$  |  | (11)\nL206:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\big(I_{j}[\\mathbf{p}^{\\prime}(\\mathbf{T}_{i},\\mathbf{T}_{j},d,\\mathbf{c})]\\!-\\!b_{j}\\big)-\\frac{t_{j}e^{a_{j}}}{t_{i}e^{a_{i}}}\\bigl(I_{i}[\\mathbf{p}]\\!-\\!b_{i}\\bigr),$  |\nL207: where $(\\mathbf{T}_{i},\\mathbf{T}_{j},d,\\mathbf{c},a_{i},a_{j},b_{i},b_{j}):=\\boldsymbol{x}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$ are the current state variables the residual depends on. The Jacobian $\\mathbf{J}_{k}$ is evaluated with respect to an additive increment to $\\boldsymbol{x}$, i.e.,\nL208: \nL209:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{J}_{k}=\\frac{\\partial r_{k}((\\boldsymbol{\\delta}+\\boldsymbol{x}){\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0})}{\\partial\\boldsymbol{\\delta}}.$  |  | (12)\nL210: It can be decomposed as\nL211:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{J}_{k}=\\biggl[\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial I_{j}}{\\partial\\mathbf{p}^{\\prime}}}_{\\mathbf{J}_{I}}\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{p}^{\\prime}((\\boldsymbol{\\delta}\\!+\\!\\boldsymbol{x}){\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0})}{\\partial\\boldsymbol{\\delta}_{\\text{geo}}}}_{\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{geo}}},~~\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial r_{k}((\\boldsymbol{\\delta}\\!+\\!\\boldsymbol{x}){\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0})}{\\partial\\boldsymbol{\\delta}_{\\text{photo}}}}_{\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{photo}}}\\biggr],$  |  | (13)\nL212: where $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}_{\\text{geo}}$ denotes the “geometric” parameters $(\\mathbf{T}_{i},\\mathbf{T}_{j},d,\\mathbf{c)}$, and $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}_{\\text{photo}}$ denotes the “photometric” parameters $(a_{i},a_{j},b_{i},b_{j})$. We employ two approximations, described below.\nL213: First, both $\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{photo}}$ and $\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{geo}}$ are evaluated at $\\boldsymbol{x}=0$. This technique is called “First Estimate Jacobians” [cite79†17 , cite95†11 ], and is required to maintain consistency of the system and prevent the accumulation of spurious information.\nL214: In particular, in the presence of non-linear null-spaces in the energy (in our formulation absolute pose and scale), adding linearizations around different evaluation points eliminates these and thus slowly corrupts the system. In practice, this approximation is very good, since $\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{photo}}$, $\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{geo}}$ are smooth compared to the size of the increment $\\boldsymbol{x}$. In contrast, $\\mathbf{J}_{I}$ is much less smooth, but does not affect the null-spaces.\nL215: Thus, it is evaluated at the current value for $\\boldsymbol{x}$, i.e., at the same point as the residual $r_{k}$. We use centred differences to compute the image derivatives at integer positions, which are then bilinearly interpolated.\nL216: Second, $\\mathbf{J}_{\\text{geo}}$ is assumed to be the same for all residuals belonging to the same point, and evaluated only for the center pixel. Again, this approximation is very good in practice. While it significantly reduces the required computations, we have not observed a notable effect on accuracy for any of the used datasets.\nL217: \nL218: From the resulting linear system, an increment is computed as $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}=\\mathbf{H}^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$ and added to the current state:\nL219:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{x}^{\\text{new}}\\leftarrow\\boldsymbol{\\delta}+\\boldsymbol{x}.$  |  | (14)\nL220: Note that due to the First Estimate Jacobian approximation, a multiplicative formulation (replacing $(\\boldsymbol{\\delta}\\!+\\!\\boldsymbol{x}){\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$ with $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}(\\boldsymbol{x}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0})$ in (cite96†12 )) results in the exact same Jacobian, thus a multiplicative update step $\\boldsymbol{x}^{\\text{new}}\\leftarrow\\log(\\boldsymbol{\\delta}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}e^{\\boldsymbol{x}})$ is equally valid.\nL221: After each update step, we update $\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$ for all variables that are not part of the marginalization term, using $\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}^{\\text{new}}~\\leftarrow~\\boldsymbol{x}~{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}~\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$ and $\\boldsymbol{x}~\\leftarrow 0$. In practice, this includes all depth values, as well as the pose of the newest keyframe.\nL222: Each time a new keyframe is added, we perform up to 6 Gauss-Newton iterations, breaking early if $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}$ is sufficiently small. We found that – since we never start far-away from the minimum – a Levenberg-Marquad dampening (which slows down convergence) is not required.\nL223: Figure 6: Windowed optimization. The red curve denotes the parameter space, composed of non-Euclidean camera poses in $\\text{SE}(3)$, and the remaining Euclidean parameters. The blue line corresponds to the tangent-space around $\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$, in which we (1) accumulate the quadratic marginalization-prior on $\\boldsymbol{x}$, and (2) compute Gauss-Newton steps $\\boldsymbol{\\delta}$. For each parameter, the tangent space is fixed as soon as that parameter becomes part of the marginalization term.\nL224: Note that while we treat all parameters equally in our notation, for Euclidean parameters tangent-space and parameter-space coincide.\nL225: ##### Marginalization.\nL226: \nL227: When the active set of variables becomes too large, old variables are removed by marginalization using the Schur complement. Similar to [cite79†17 ], we drop any residual terms that would affect the sparsity pattern of $\\mathbf{H}$: When marginalizing frame $i$, we first marginalize all points in $\\mathcal{P}_{i}$, as well as points that have not been observed in the last two keyframes. Remaining observations of active points in frame $i$ are dropped from the system.\nL228: Marginalization proceeds as follows: Let $E^{\\prime}$ denote the part of the energy containing all residuals that depend on state variables to be marginalized. We first compute a Gauss-Newton approximation of $E^{\\prime}$ around the current state estimate $\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}=\\boldsymbol{x}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$. This gives\nL229:  |  | $\\displaystyle E^{\\prime}(\\boldsymbol{x}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0})$  |  | (15)\nL230:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\approx 2(\\boldsymbol{x}\\!-\\!\\boldsymbol{x}_{0})^{T}\\mathbf{b}+(\\boldsymbol{x}\\!-\\!\\boldsymbol{x}_{0})^{T}\\mathbf{H}(\\boldsymbol{x}\\!-\\!\\boldsymbol{x}_{0})+c$  |\nL231:  |  | $\\displaystyle=2\\boldsymbol{x}^{T}\\underbrace{(\\mathbf{b}\\!-\\!\\mathbf{H}\\boldsymbol{x}_{0})}_{=:\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}}+\\boldsymbol{x}^{T}\\mathbf{H}\\boldsymbol{x}+\\underbrace{(c\\!+\\!\\boldsymbol{x}_{0}^{T}\\mathbf{H}\\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\\!-\\!\\boldsymbol{x}_{0}^{T}\\mathbf{b})}_{=:c^{\\prime}},$  |\nL232: where $\\boldsymbol{x}_{0}$ denotes the current value (evaluation point for $\\mathbf{r}$) of $\\boldsymbol{x}$. The constants $c,c^{\\prime}$ can be dropped, and $\\mathbf{H},\\mathbf{b}$ are defined as in (cite97†10 -cite98†13 ). This is a quadratic function on $\\boldsymbol{x}$, and we can apply the Schur complement to marginalize a subset of variables. Written as a linear system, it becomes\nL233:  | $\\displaystyle\\begin{bmatrix}\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\alpha}&\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\\\\nL234: \\mathbf{H}_{\\beta\\alpha}&\\mathbf{H}_{\\beta\\beta}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{{\\boldsymbol{x}}}_{\\alpha}\\\\\nL235: {{\\boldsymbol{x}}}_{\\beta}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\alpha}\\\\\nL236: \\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\beta}\\end{bmatrix},$  |  | (16)\nL237: where $\\beta$ denotes the block of variables we would like to marginalize, and $\\alpha$ the block of variables we would like to keep. Applying the Schur complement yields $\\widehat{\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\alpha}}\\boldsymbol{x}_{\\alpha}=\\widehat{\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\alpha}}$, with\nL238:  | $\\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\alpha}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\alpha}-\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\mathbf{H}_{\\beta\\beta}^{-1}\\mathbf{H}_{\\beta\\alpha}$  |  | (17)\nL239:  | $\\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\alpha}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\alpha}-\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\mathbf{H}_{\\beta\\beta}^{-1}\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\beta}.$  |  | (18)\nL240: \nL241: The residual energy on $\\boldsymbol{x}_{\\alpha}$ can hence be written as\nL242:  | $\\displaystyle E^{\\prime}\\!\\big(\\boldsymbol{x}_{\\alpha}{\\,\\scriptscriptstyle\\boxplus\\,}(\\!{\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}}\\!)_{\\alpha}\\big)=2\\boldsymbol{x}_{\\alpha}^{T}\\widehat{\\mathbf{b}^{\\prime}_{\\alpha}}+\\boldsymbol{x}_{\\alpha}^{T}\\widehat{\\mathbf{H}_{\\alpha\\alpha}}\\boldsymbol{x}_{\\alpha}.$  |  | (19)\nL243: This is a quadratic function on $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and can be trivially added to the full photometric error $E_{\\text{photo}}$ during all subsequent optimization and marginalization operations, replacing the corresponding non-linear terms. Note that this requires the tangent space for $\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}_{0}$ to remain the same for all variables that appear in $E^{\\prime}$ during all subsequent optimization and marginalization steps.\nL244: ## 3 Visual Odometry Front-End\nL245: \nL246: The front end is the part of the algorithm that\nL247: \nL248:   * •\nL249: \nL250: determines the sets $\\mathcal{F},\\mathcal{P}_{i}$, and $\\text{obs}(\\mathbf{p})$ that make up the error terms of $E_{\\text{photo}}$. It decides which points and frames are used, and in which frames a point is visible – in particular, this includes outlier removal and occlusion detection.\nL251: \nL252:   * •\nL253: provides initializations for new parameters, required for optimizing the highly non-convex energy function $E_{\\text{photo}}$. As a rule of thumb, a linearization of the image $I$ is only valid in a 1-2 pixel radius; hence all parameters involved in computing $\\mathbf{p}^{\\prime}$ should be initialized sufficiently accurately for $\\mathbf{p}^{\\prime}$ to be off by no more than 1-2 pixels.\nL254: \nL255:   * •\nL256: \nL257: decides when a point / frame should be marginalized.\nL258: As such, the front-end needs to replace many operations that in the indirect setting are accomplished by keyframe detectors (determining visibility, point selection) and initialization procedures such as RANSAC. Note that many procedures described here are specific to the monocular case. For instance, using a stereo camera makes obtaining initial depth values more straightforward, while integration of an IMU can significantly robustify – or even directly provide – a pose initialization for new frames.\nL259: ### 3.1 Frame Management\nL260: \nL261: Our method always keeps a window of up to $N_{f}$ active keyframes (we use $N_{f}=7$). Every new frame is initially tracked with respect to these reference frames (Step 1). It is then either discarded or used to create a new keyframe (Step 2). Once a new keyframe – and respective new points – are created, the total photometric error (cite92†8 ) is optimized. Afterwards, we marginalize one or more frames (Step 3).\nL262: \nL263: cite99†Image: Refer to caption L264: \nL265: cite100†Image: Refer to caption L266: cite101†Image: Refer to caption L267: \nL268: cite102†Image: Refer to caption L269: \nL270: cite103†Image: Refer to caption L271: \nL272: cite104†Image: Refer to caption L273: \nL274: cite105†Image: Refer to caption L275: \nL276: cite106†Image: Refer to caption L277: Figure 7: Example depth maps used for initial frame tracking. The top row shows the original images, the bottom row the color-coded depth maps. Since we aim at a fixed number of points in the active optimization, they become more sparse in densely textured scenes (left), while becoming similar in density to those of LSD-SLAM in scenes where only few informative image regions are available to sample from (right).\nL278: ##### Step 1: Initial Frame Tracking.\nL279: When a new keyframe is created, all active points are projected into it and slightly dilated, creating a semi-dense depth map. New frames are tracked with respect to only this frame using conventional two-frame direct image alignment, a multi-scale image pyramid and a constant motion model to initialize. Figure cite107†7 shows some examples – we found that further increasing the density has little to no benefit in terms of accuracy or robustness, while significantly increasing runtime.\nL280: Note that when down-scaling the images, a pixel is assigned a depth value if at least one of the source pixels has a depth value as in [cite108†24 ], significantly increasing the density on coarser resolutions.\nL281: If the final RMSE for a frame is more than twice that of the frame before, we assume that direct image alignment failed and attempt to recover by initializing with up to 27 different small rotations in different directions. This recovery-tracking is done on the coarsest pyramid level only, and takes approximately 0.5ms per try. Note that this RANSAC-like procedure is only rarely invoked, such as when the camera moves very quickly shakily. Tightly integrating an IMU would likely render this unnecessary.\nL282: ##### Step 2: Keyframe Creation.\nL283: \nL284: Similar to ORB-SLAM, our strategy is to initially take many keyframes (around 5-10 keyframes per second), and sparsify them afterwards by early marginalizing redundant keyframes. We combine three criteria to determine if a new keyframe is required:\nL285: \nL286:   1. 1.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/JakobEngel/dso/master/src/FullSystem/FullSystemOptimize.cpp)\nciteturn290view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn288view3\",\"pattern\":\"accept\"}); Total lines: 640\nL279: // sets linearization point.\nL280: void FullSystem::backupState(bool backupLastStep)\nL301: \t\t\tHcalib.step_backup.setZero();\nL302: \t\t\tHcalib.value_backup = Hcalib.value;\nL303: \t\t\tfor(FrameHessian* fh : frameHessians)\nL304: \t\t\t{\nL305: \t\t\t\tfh->step_backup.setZero();\nL306: \t\t\t\tfh->state_backup = fh->get_state();\nL307: \t\t\t\tfor(PointHessian* ph : fh->pointHessians)\nL308: \t\t\t\t{\nL309: \t\t\t\t\tph->idepth_backup = ph->idepth;\nL310: \t\t\t\t\tph->step_backup=0;\nL311: \t\t\t\t}\nL312: \t\t\t}\nL313: \t\t}\nL314: \t}\nL315: \telse\nL316: \t{\nL317: \t\tHcalib.value_backup = Hcalib.value;\nL318: \t\tfor(FrameHessian* fh : frameHessians)\nL319: \t\t{\nL320: \t\t\tfh->state_backup = fh->get_state();\nL321: \t\t\tfor(PointHessian* ph : fh->pointHessians)\nL322: \t\t\t\tph->idepth_backup = ph->idepth;\nL323: \t\t}\nL324: \t}\nL325: }\nL326: // sets linearization point.\nL327: void FullSystem::loadSateBackup()\nL328: {\nL329: \tHcalib.setValue(Hcalib.value_backup);\nL330: \tfor(FrameHessian* fh : frameHessians)\nL331: \t{\nL332: \t\tfh->setState(fh->state_backup);\nL333: \t\tfor(PointHessian* ph : fh->pointHessians)\nL334: \t\t{\nL335: \t\t\tph->setIdepth(ph->idepth_backup);\nL336: \nL337:             ph->setIdepthZero(ph->idepth_backup);\nL338: \t\t}\nL339: \nL340: \t}\nL341: \nL342: \nL343: \tEFDeltaValid=false;\nL344: \tsetPrecalcValues();\nL345: }\nL346: \nL347: double FullSystem::calcMEnergy()\nL348: {\nL349: \tif(setting_forceAceptStep) return 0;\nL350: \t// calculate (x-x0)^T * [2b + H * (x-x0)] for everything saved in L.\nL351: \t//ef->makeIDX();\nL352: \t//ef->setDeltaF(&Hcalib);\nL353: \treturn ef->calcMEnergyF();\nL354: \nL355: }\nL356: \nL357: void FullSystem::printOptRes(const Vec3 &res, double resL, double resM, double resPrior, double LExact, float a, float b)\nL358: {\nL359: \tprintf(\"A(%f)=(AV %.3f). Num: A(%'d) + M(%'d); ab %f %f!\\n\",\nL360: \t\t\tres[0],\nL361: \t\t\tsqrtf((float)(res[0] / (patternNum*ef->resInA))),\nL362: \t\t\tef->resInA,\nL363: \t\t\tef->resInM,\nL364: \t\t\ta,\nL365: \t\t\tb\nL366: \t);\nL367: \nL368: }\nL369: \nL370: \nL371: float FullSystem::optimize(int mnumOptIts)\nL372: {\nL373: \nL374: \tif(frameHessians.size() < 2) return 0;\nL375: \tif(frameHessians.size() < 3) mnumOptIts = 20;\nL376: \tif(frameHessians.size() < 4) mnumOptIts = 15;\nL377: \nL378: \t// get statistics and active residuals.\nL379: \nL380: \tactiveResiduals.clear();\nL381: \tint numPoints = 0;\nL382: \tint numLRes = 0;\nL383: \tfor(FrameHessian* fh : frameHessians)\nL384: \t\tfor(PointHessian* ph : fh->pointHessians)\nL385: \t\t{\nL386: \t\t\tfor(PointFrameResidual* r : ph->residuals)\nL387: \t\t\t{\nL388: \t\t\t\tif(!r->efResidual->isLinearized)\nL389: \t\t\t\t{\nL390: \t\t\t\t\tactiveResiduals.push_back(r);\nL391: \t\t\t\t\tr->resetOOB();\nL392: \t\t\t\t}\nL393: \t\t\t\telse\nL394: \t\t\t\t\tnumLRes++;\nL395: \t\t\t}\nL396: \t\t\tnumPoints++;\nL397: \t\t}\nL398:     if(!setting_debugout_runquiet)\nL399:         printf(\"OPTIMIZE %d pts, %d active res, %d lin res!\\n\",ef